#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Introduction {#1-introduction}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Bits, gates, and circuits are the basic building blocks of quantum "
"computing. You will learn quantum computation with the circuit model using "
"quantum bits and gates, and also review the superposition, measurement, and "
"entanglement.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this lesson you will learn:\n"
"- Single-qubit gates\n"
"- Bloch sphere\n"
"- Superposition\n"
"- Measurement\n"
"- Two-qubit gates and entanglement state\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"At the end of this lecture, you will learn about circuit depth, which is "
"essential for utility-scale quantum computing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Computation as a diagram {#2-computation-as-a-diagram}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When using qubits or bits, we need to manipulate them in order to turn the "
"inputs we have into the outputs we need. For the simplest programs with very"
" few bits, it is useful to represent this process in a diagram known as a "
"*circuit diagram*.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The bottom-left figure is an example of a classical circuit, and the bottom-"
"right figure is an example of a quantum circuit. In both cases, the inputs "
"are on the left and the outputs are on the right, while the operations are "
"represented by symbols. The symbols used for the operations are called "
"“gates”, mostly for historical reasons.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![\"classical logic and quantum circuit\"](/learning/images/courses/utility-"
"scale-quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/classical-vs-quantum.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. Single-qubit quantum gate {#3-single-qubit-quantum-gate}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"3.1 Quantum state and Bloch sphere {#31-quantum-state-and-bloch-sphere}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A qubit's state is represented as a superposition of $|0\\rangle$ and "
"$|1\\rangle$. An arbitrary quantum state is represented as\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi\\rangle =\\alpha|0\\rangle+ \\beta|1\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are complex numbers such that "
"$|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$ are vectors in the two-dimensional complex "
"vector space:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|0\\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 \\\\0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix},\n"
"|1\\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0\\\\1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Therefore, an arbitrary quantum state is also represented as\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi\\rangle = \\alpha\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 \\\\ 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix} + \\beta\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\\n"
"1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\alpha \\\\ \\beta\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"From this, we can see that the state of a quantum bit is a unit vector in a "
"two-dimensional complex inner product space with an orthonormal basis of "
"$|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$. It is normalized to 1.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle\\psi|\\psi\\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\alpha^* & \\beta^*\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\alpha \\\\\n"
"\\beta\n"
"\\end{pmatrix} = 1\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ |\\psi\\rangle =\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\alpha \\\\ \\beta\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}$ is also called the statevector.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A single-qubit quantum state is also represented as\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi\\rangle\n"
"=\\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}|0\\rangle+e^{i\\varphi}\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}|1\\rangle\n"
"=\\left( \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\\\ e^{i\\varphi}\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\\right)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $\\theta$ and $\\varphi$ are the angles of the Bloch sphere in the "
"following figure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Bloch sphere](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/bloch.avif)\n"
"In the next few code cells, we will build up basic calculations from constituent pieces in Qiskit. We'll construct an empty circuit and then add quantum operations, discussing the gates and visualizing their effects as we go.\n"
"You can run the cell by \"Shift\" + \"Enter\". Import the libraries first.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Prepare the quantum circuit {#prepare-the-quantum-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "We will create and draw a single-qubit circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/fdef9137-9e39-4b05-afa9-9091766053f6-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "X gate {#x-gate}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The X gate is a $\\pi$ rotation around the $x$ axis of the Bloch sphere.\n"
"Applying the X gate to $|0\\rangle$ results in $|1\\rangle$, and applying the X gate to $|1\\rangle$ results in $|0\\rangle$, so it is an operation similar to the classical NOT gate, and is also known as bit flip. The matrix representation of the X gate is below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"X = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1 \\\\\n"
"1 & 0 \\\\\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/b3468430-0cbc-44ab-bd21-901436662a4b-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In IBM Quantum&reg;, the initial state is set to $|0\\rangle$, so the "
"quantum circuit above in matrix representation is\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"X|0\\rangle=  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1 \\\\\n"
"1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 \\\\ 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
" =\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 \\\\ 1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix} = |1\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Next, let's run this circuit using a statevector simulator.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/efb727e8-f51e-4903-aa0f-e4c099316815-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Vertical vector is displayed as row vector, with complex numbers (the "
"imaginary part is indexed by $j$ ).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "H gate {#h-gate}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Hadamard gate is a $\\pi$ rotation around an axis halfway between the "
"$x$ and $z$ axes on the Bloch sphere. Applying the H gate to $|0\\rangle$ "
"creates a superposition state such as $\\frac{|0\\rangle + "
"|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}$. The matrix representation of the H gate is below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"H = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 1 \\\\\n"
"1 & -1 \\\\\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/574c35f0-26a9-423a-8d65-7140ba7398e3-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/7adf0e93-6db3-4aa8-800f-c91c8cc46001-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is\n"
"$$\n"
"H|0\\rangle= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 1 \\\\\n"
"1 & -1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 \\\\0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
" =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 \\\\\n"
"1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"=\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0.707 \\\\\n"
"0.707\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This superposition state is so common and important, that it is given its "
"own symbol:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|+\\rangle \\equiv \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle).\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"By applying the $H$ gate on the $|0\\rangle$, we created a superposition of "
"$|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$ where measurement in the computational basis "
"(along z in the Bloch sphere picture) would give you each state with equal "
"probabilities.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$|-\\rangle$ state {#rangle-state}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You might have guessed that there is a corresponding  $|-\\rangle$ state:\n"
"$$\n"
"|-\\rangle \\equiv \\frac{|0\\rangle -|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n"
"$$\n"
"To create this state, first apply an X gate to make $|1\\rangle$, then apply an H gate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/b8e96fc4-6708-415e-b8fc-ac69eaeae750-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/f42b82aa-7a15-4f81-8530-b997d4c6e0de-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"H|1\\rangle= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 1 \\\\\\\n"
"1 & -1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 \\\\\\\n"
"1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
" =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 \\\\\\\n"
"-1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"=\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0.707 \\\\\\\n"
"-0.707\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle) = |-\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Applying the $H$ gate on $|1\\rangle$ results in an equal superposition of "
"$|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$, but the sign of $|1\\rangle$ is negative.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"3.2 Single-qubit quantum state and unitary evolution {#32-single-qubit-"
"quantum-state-and-unitary-evolution}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The actions of all the gates we have seen so far have been *unitary*, which "
"means they can be represented by a unitary operator. In other words, the "
"output state can be obtained by acting on the initial state with a unitary "
"matrix:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi^{'}\\rangle = U|\\psi\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A unitary matrix is a matrix satisfying\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"U^{\\dagger}U =U U^{\\dagger} = I.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In terms of quantum computer operation, we would say that applying a quantum"
" gate to the qubit evolves the quantum state. Common single-qubit gates "
"include the following.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pauli gates:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"X = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1 \\\\\n"
"1 & 0 \\\\\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= |0\\rangle \\langle 1|+|1\\rangle \\langle 0|\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"Y = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & -i \\\\\n"
"i & 0 \\\\\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= -i|0\\rangle \\langle 1|+i|1\\rangle \\langle 0|\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"Z = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0 \\\\\n"
"0 & -1 \\\\\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= |0\\rangle \\langle 0|-|1\\rangle \\langle 1|\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where the outer product was calculated as follows:\n"
"$$\n"
"|0\\rangle \\langle 0|=\n"
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 \\\\\n"
"0\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}\n"
"=\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0 \\\\\n"
"0 & 0 \\\\\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad\n"
"|1\\rangle \\langle 0|=\n"
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"0 \\\\\n"
"1\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}\n"
"=\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"0 & 0 \\\\\n"
"1 & 0 \\\\\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|0\\rangle \\langle 1|=\n"
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 \\\\\n"
"0\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}\n"
"=\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1 \\\\\n"
"0 & 0 \\\\\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad\n"
"|1\\rangle \\langle 1|=\n"
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"0 \\\\\n"
"1\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}\n"
"=\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"0 & 0 \\\\\n"
"0 & 1 \\\\\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Other typical single-qubit gates:\n"
"$$\n"
"H= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 & 1 \\\\\n"
"1 & -1 \\\\\n"
"\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\n"
"S = \\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0 \\\\\n"
"0 & i \\\\\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad\n"
"T = \\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0 \\\\\n"
"0 & exp(i\\pi/4) \\\\\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"R_x(\\theta) = e^{-i\\theta X/2} = cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}I - i sin \\frac{\\theta}{2}X = \\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"cos\\frac{\\theta}{2} &  -i sin \\frac{\\theta}{2} \\\\\n"
"-i sin \\frac{\\theta}{2} & cos\\frac{\\theta}{2} \\\\\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"R_y(\\theta) = e^{-i\\theta Y/2} = cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}I - i sin \\frac{\\theta}{2}Y = \\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"cos\\frac{\\theta}{2} &  - sin \\frac{\\theta}{2} \\\\\n"
"sin \\frac{\\theta}{2} & cos\\frac{\\theta}{2} \\\\\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"R_z(\\theta) = e^{-i\\theta Z/2} = cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}I - i sin \\frac{\\theta}{2}Z = \\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"e^{-i\\theta /2} &  0 \\\\\n"
"0 & e^{i\\theta /2} \\\\\n"
"\\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The meaning and use of these are described in more detail in the [Basics of "
"Quantum Information](/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information) "
"course.\n"
msgstr ""

