#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3.3 Measurement {#33-measurement}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Measurement is theoretically a very complicated topic. But in practical terms, making a measurement along $z$ (as all IBM&reg; quantum computers do) simply forces the qubit’s state $\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle \\quad (s.t.|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1)$ either to $|0\\rangle$ or to $|1\\rangle$, and we observe the outcome.\n"
"- $|\\alpha|^2$ is the probability we will get $|0\\rangle$ when we measure.\n"
"- $|\\beta|^2$ is the probability we will get $|1\\rangle$ when we measure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"So, $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are called probability amplitudes. (see \"Born "
"rule\")\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For example, $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|0\\rangle+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|1\\rangle$"
" has an equal probability of becoming $|0\\rangle$ or $|1\\rangle$ upon "
"measurement. $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}|0\\rangle-\\frac{1}{2}i|1\\rangle$ has a "
"75% chance of becoming $|0\\rangle$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Qiskit Aer Simulator {#qiskit-aer-simulator}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, let's measure a circuit that prepares the equal probability superposition above.\n"
"We should add the measurement gates, as the Qiskit Aer simulator simulates an ideal (with no noise) quantum hardware by default. Note: The Aer simulator can also apply a noise model based on real quantum computer. We will return to noise models later.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/3956e602-9f1b-4083-b58d-26c91b50b75e-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We are now ready to run our circuit on the Aer simulator. In this example, "
"we will apply the default shots=1024, which means we will measure 1024 "
"times. Then we will plot those counts in a histogram.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/7cb7c5ab-7b12-4f77-aaca-1d7e6cff213c-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We see that 0s and 1s were measured with a probability of almost 50% each. "
"Although noise has not been simulated here, the states are still "
"probabilistic. So while we expect roughly a 50-50 distribution, we will "
"rarely find exactly that. Just as 100 flips of a coin would rarely yield "
"exactly 50 instances of each side.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"4. Multi-qubit quantum gate and entanglement {#4-multi-qubit-quantum-gate-"
"and-entanglement}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4.1 Multi-qubit quantum circuit {#41-multi-qubit-quantum-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can create a two-qubit quantum circuit with following code. We will apply"
" an H gate to each qubit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/e880f7eb-108c-4387-8ef4-97219fd5691c-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Note: Qiskit bit ordering {#note-qiskit-bit-ordering}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Qiskit uses **Little Endian** notation when ordering qubits and bits, "
"meaning **qubit 0 is the rightmost bit** in the bitstrings. Example: "
"$|01\\rangle$ means q0 is $|1\\rangle$ and q1 is $|0\\rangle$. Be careful "
"because some literature in quantum computing use the Big Endian notation "
"(qubit 0 is the leftmost bit) and a great deal of quantum mechanics "
"literature does, too.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Another thing to notice is that when representing a quantum circuit, $|q_0\\rangle$ is always placed at the top of the circuit.\n"
"With this in mind, the quantum state of the above circuit can be written as a tensor product of single-qubit quantum state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$\n"
"|q1\\rangle \\otimes|q0\\rangle = (a|0\\rangle+b|1\\rangle) \\otimes (c|0\\rangle+d|1\\rangle)\n"
"$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$\n"
"= ac|0\\rangle|0\\rangle+ad|0\\rangle|1\\rangle+bc|1\\rangle|0\\rangle+bd|1\\rangle|1\\rangle\n"
"$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$\n"
"= ac|00\\rangle+ad|01\\rangle+bc|10\\rangle+bd|11\\rangle\n"
"$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "( $|ac|^2+ |ad|^2+ |bc|^2+ |bd|^2=1$ )\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The initial state of Qiskit is $|0\\rangle|0\\rangle=|00\\rangle$, so by "
"applying $H$ to each qubit, it changes to a state of equal superposition.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$H|0\\rangle \\otimes "
"H|0\\rangle=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle) \\otimes "
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle) = "
"\\frac{1}{2}(|00\\rangle+|01\\rangle+|10\\rangle+|11\\rangle)$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"=\\frac{1}{2}\\left( \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\otimes \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}+\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}+\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}+\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\right)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The measurement rule is also same as a single qubit case, the probability of"
" measuring $|00\\rangle$ is $|ac|^2$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/ebdf591f-18d0-4fd1-a3a3-b1cdac13bd97-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Next, let's measure this circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/2afdd5ac-e51a-4d94-9307-955cc8fbf821-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now, we will use a Aer simulator, again, to experimentally verify that the "
"relative probabilities of all possible output states are roughly equal.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/060512ac-a106-4ecd-94f5-119048a08467-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As expected, the states $|00\\rangle$, $|01\\rangle$, $|10\\rangle$, "
"$|11\\rangle$ were measured almost 25% each.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4.2 Multi-qubit quantum gates {#42-multi-qubit-quantum-gates}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "CNOT gate {#cnot-gate}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A CNOT(\"controlled NOT\" or CX) gate is a two-qubit gate, meaning its "
"action involves two qubits at once: the control qubit and the target qubit. "
"A CNOT flips the target qubit only when the control qubit is $|1\\rangle$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Input (target,control) | Output (target,control) |\n"
"|:-----------:|:------------:|\n"
"| 00          | 00           |\n"
"| 01          | 11           |\n"
"| 10          | 10           |\n"
"| 11          | 01           |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let us first simulate the action of this two-qubit gate when q0 and q1 are "
"both $|0\\rangle$, and obtain the output statevector. The Qiskit syntax used"
" is ```qc.cx(control qubit, target qubit)```.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-outputs/a5621efd-"
"dd9f-4ea4-bf3a-be3cacb45287-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As expected, applying a CNOT gate on $|00\\rangle$ did not change the state, since the control qubit was in the $|0\\rangle$ state.\n"
"Let's get back to our CNOT operation. This time we will apply a CNOT gate to $|01\\rangle$ and see what happens.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/5a02de82-44ff-40eb-b64c-2389d6eae3ee-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"By applying a CNOT gate, the $|01\\rangle$ state has now become "
"$|11\\rangle$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Let us verify these results by running the circuit on a simulator.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/abcafb77-20bf-4bb7-bf8d-57f33af280c1-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/88b76f1d-083b-4f28-b415-380738d22599-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The results should show you that $|11\\rangle$ has been measured with 100% "
"probability.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"4.3 Quantum entanglement and execution on a real quantum device "
"{#43-quantum-entanglement-and-execution-on-a-real-quantum-device}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let's start by introducing a specific entangled state which is particularly "
"important in quantum computation, then we'll define the term "
"\"entangled\":\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|00\\rangle + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|11\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
"and this state is called a **Bell state**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"An entangled state is a state $|\\psi_{AB}\\rangle$ consisting of quantum "
"states $|\\psi_A\\rangle$ and $|\\psi_B\\rangle$ that cannot be represented "
"by a tensor product of individual quantum states.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If $|\\psi_{AB}\\rangle$ below has two states $|\\psi\\rangle_A$ and "
"$|\\psi\\rangle_B$;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi_{AB}\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|00\\rangle +|11\\rangle) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle_A|0\\rangle_B +|1\\rangle_A|1\\rangle_B)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi\\rangle_A = a_0|0\\rangle+a_1|1\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\psi\\rangle_B = b_0|0\\rangle+b_1|1\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "the tensor product of these two states is the following\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi\\rangle _A\\otimes |\\psi\\rangle _B = a_0 b_0|00\\rangle+a_0 b_1|01\\rangle+a_1 b_0|10\\rangle+a_1 b_1|11\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"but there are no coefficients $a_0, a_1, b_0, $ and $b_1$ to satisfy these "
"two equations. Therefore, $|\\psi_{AB}\\rangle$ is not represented by a "
"tensor product of individual quantum state, $|\\psi\\rangle_A$ and "
"$|\\psi\\rangle_B$, and this means that $|\\psi_{AB}\\rangle =  "
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|00\\rangle +|11\\rangle)$ is entangled state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let us create the Bell state and run it on a real quantum computer. Now we "
"will follow the four steps to writing a quantum program, called **Qiskit "
"patterns**:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '    1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Map problem to quantum circuits and operators\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '    2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Optimize for target hardware\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '    3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Execute on target hardware\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '    4. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Post-process the results\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1. Map problem to quantum circuits and operators {#step-1-map-problem-"
"to-quantum-circuits-and-operators}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In a quantum program, quantum circuits are the native format in which to "
"represent quantum instructions. When creating a circuit, you'll usually "
"create a new QuantumCircuit object, then add instructions to it in "
"sequence.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following code cell creates a circuit that produces a Bell state, the "
"specific two-qubit entangled state from above.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/5fdd9317-8fab-46ee-ac6d-25ef031dd52b-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 2. Optimize for target hardware {#step-2-optimize-for-target-hardware}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Qiskit converts abstract circuits to QISA (Quantum Instruction Set Architecture) circuits that respect the constraints of the target hardware and optimizes circuit performance. So before the optimization, we will specify the target hardware.\n"
"If you do not have `qiskit-ibm-runtime`, you will need to install this first. For more information, [check out the API reference](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-ibm-runtime/runtime-service).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "We will specify the target hardware.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Transpiling the circuit is yet another complex process. Very briefly, this "
"rewrites the circuit into a logically equivalent one using \"native gates\" "
"(gates that a particular quantum computer can implement) and maps the qubits"
" in your circuit to optimal real qubits on the target quantum computer. For "
"more on transpilation, see this "
"[documentation](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/transpiler#overview).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can see that in the transpilation the circuit was rewritten using new "
"gates. For more information, refer to the "
"[ECRGate](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.library.ECRGate#ecrgate)"
" documentation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 3. Execute the target circuit {#step-3-execute-the-target-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Now, we will run the target circuit on the real device.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Execution on the real device might require waiting in a queue, since quantum"
" computers are valuable resources, and very much in demand. The job_id is "
"used to check the execution status and results of the job later.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can also check the job status from your IBM Quantum "
"dashboard：https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/workloads\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 4. Post-process the results {#step-4-post-process-the-results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, we must post-process our results to create outputs in the expected "
"format, like values or graphs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As you can see, $|00\\rangle$ and $|11\\rangle$ are the most frequently "
"observed. There are a few results other than the expected data, and they are"
" due to noise and qubit decoherence. We will learn more about errors and "
"noise in quantum computers in the later lessons of this course.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4.4 GHZ state {#44-ghz-state}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The concept of entanglement can be extended to systems of more than two "
"qubits. The GHZ state (Greenberger-Horne-Zeilinger state) is a maximally "
"entangled state of three or more qubits. The GHZ state for three qubits is "
"defined as\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt 2}(|000\\rangle + |111\\rangle)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It can be created with the following quantum circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/utility-scale-"
"quantum-computing/bits-gates-and-circuits/extracted-"
"outputs/459b6162-3a54-41fe-9b67-9c1f8e731ebc-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The \"depth\" of a quantum circuit is a useful and common metric to describe"
" quantum circuits. Trace a path through the quantum circuit, moving left to "
"right, only changing qubits when they are connected by a multi-qubit gate. "
"Count the number of gates along that path. The maximum number of gates for "
"any such path through a circuit is the depth. In modern noisy quantum "
"computers, low-depth circuits have fewer errors and are likely to return "
"good results. Very deep circuits are not.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Using `QuantumCircuit.depth()`, we can check the depth of our quantum "
"circuit. The depth of the above circuit is 4. The top qubit has only three "
"gates including the measurement. But there is a path from the top qubit down"
" to either qubit 1 or qubit 2 which involves another CNOT gate.\n"
msgstr ""

